{"task_id": "Mbpp/109", "solution": "def odd_Equivalent(binary_string: str, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of left-rotations of a binary string (up to n times) that result in an odd integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    def rotate(s: str, k: int) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Performs a left-rotation on string s by k positions.\"\"\"\n        if not s:\n            return s\n        actual_k = k % len(s)\n        return s[actual_k:] + s[:actual_k]\n\n    def is_odd(s: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Returns True if the binary string represents an odd number (i.e., its least significant bit is '1').\"\"\"\n        if not s:\n            return False\n        return s[-1] == '1'\n\n    odd_count = 0\n    current_string = binary_string\n    # The problem implies we check `n` rotations.\n    # A rotation by 0 positions is the original string.\n    # Subsequent rotations are by 1, 2, ..., n-1 positions relative to the original.\n    # Or, equivalently, we rotate the `current_string` once for each step.\n    \n    # If we iterate k from 0 to n-1, `rotate(binary_string, k)` would be used.\n    # If we rotate the `current_string` repeatedly, then the loop becomes simpler.\n    # The wording \"rotating a binary string the given number of times\" can be ambiguous.\n    # \"find the number of numbers with an odd value when rotating a binary string the given number of times.\"\n    # The example odd_Equivalent(\"011001\",6) == 3 suggests 6 different states from k=0 to k=5 rotations.\n\n    for k in range(n):\n        rotated_string = rotate(binary_string, k)\n        if is_odd(rotated_string):\n            odd_count += 1\n            \n    return odd_count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/111", "solution": "class CommonElementsFinder:\n    \"\"\"\n    A domain service to find common elements across multiple lists.\n    It encapsulates the logic for set intersection.\n    \"\"\"\n    def find_common_elements(self, nested_list):\n        \"\"\"\n        Finds elements common to all sublists in a given nested list.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not nested_list:\n            return []\n\n        # Convert the first sublist to a set to initialize the common elements\n        common_elements_set = set(nested_list[0])\n\n        # Iterate through the rest of the sublists and perform intersection\n        for sublist in nested_list[1:]:\n            current_sublist_set = set(sublist)\n            common_elements_set = common_elements_set.intersection(current_sublist_set)\n            # If at any point the intersection becomes empty, no further elements can be common\n            if not common_elements_set:\n                break\n\n        return list(common_elements_set)\ndef common_in_nested_lists(nested_list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the common elements in given nested lists by delegating to a domain service.\n    \"\"\"\n    finder = CommonElementsFinder()\n    return finder.find_common_elements(nested_list)"}
