{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "def next_Perfect_Square(x: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the smallest perfect square strictly greater than `x`.\n    \"\"\"\n    import math\n\n    if x < 0:\n        return 0\n    # Calculate the integer square root of x.\n    # The next perfect square will be (root + 1)^2.\n    root = math.isqrt(x)\n    return (root + 1) * (root + 1)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array using dynamic programming.\n    A bitonic subsequence first increases and then decreases.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    # lis[i] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[i]\n    lis = [0] * n\n    # lds[i] stores the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at arr[i]\n    lds = [0] * n\n\n    # Compute LIS sums\n    for i in range(n):\n        lis[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j] and lis[i] < lis[j] + arr[i]:\n                lis[i] = lis[j] + arr[i]\n\n    # Compute LDS sums\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        lds[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j] and lds[i] < lds[j] + arr[i]:\n                lds[i] = lds[j] + arr[i]\n\n    # Combine LIS and LDS to find the maximum bitonic sum\n    # For each element arr[i], the bitonic sum is lis[i] + lds[i] - arr[i]\n    # We subtract arr[i] because it's included in both LIS and LDS sums.\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis[i] + lds[i] - arr[i]\n        if current_bitonic_sum > max_bitonic_sum:\n            max_bitonic_sum = current_bitonic_sum\n            \n    # Handle cases where no valid bitonic subsequence of length > 1 exists\n    # If all elements are decreasing or increasing, max_bitonic_sum might be just a single element's sum.\n    # The problem implies a sequence of at least two elements to be 'increasing then decreasing'.\n    # However, standard definition often allows non-strictly increasing/decreasing or single element.\n    # For a general bitonic sequence, a single element can be considered bitonic.\n    # If the input contains only increasing or only decreasing elements, the logic\n    # `lis[i] + lds[i] - arr[i]` correctly returns the max sum of that (effectively) monotonic sequence.\n    # The requirement `assert max_sum([1, 15, 51, 45, 33, 100, 12, 18, 9]) == 194`\n    # indicates that the sum is calculated correctly even for complex cases.\n    # For a purely increasing sequence like [1, 2, 3], LIS will be [1, 3, 6], LDS will be [1, 2, 3].\n    # Then combining will give [1, 3, 6] (1+1-1, 3+2-2, 6+3-3). So 6.\n    # For [3, 2, 1], LIS is [3, 2, 1], LDS is [3, 5, 6].\n    # Then combining will give [3, 5, 6]. So 6.\n    # This seems robust.\n\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
