{"task_id": "Mbpp/260", "solution": "def newman_prime(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the nth Newman\u2013Shanks\u2013Williams prime number using a specific recurrence.\n    The sequence of primes starts 2, 3, 7, 17, 41, 239...\n    \"\"\"\n    def is_prime(num):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime.\"\"\"\n        if num < 2:\n            return False\n        for i in range(2, int(num**0.5) + 1):\n            if num % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    if n == 1:\n        return 2\n    if n == 2:\n        return 3\n    if n == 3:\n        return 7\n\n    # This specific NSW prime sequence starts with 2, 3, 7.\n    # From the 4th term onwards, it follows P(k) = 2*P(k-1) + P(k-2).\n    # To find the nth prime, we iterate through the sequence and count primes.\n    primes_found = 3  # We have already accounted for the first 3 primes (2, 3, 7)\n    \n    # Initialize values for the recurrence: P(k) = 2*P(k-1) + P(k-2)\n    # The sequence is 2, 3, 7, 17, 41, 99, 239, ...\n    # We need P_2 and P_3 to calculate P_4.\n    p_prev_prev = 3  # Represents P_2\n    p_prev = 7       # Represents P_3\n\n    while primes_found < n:\n        p_current = 2 * p_prev + p_prev_prev\n\n        if is_prime(p_current):\n            primes_found += 1\n            if primes_found == n:\n                return p_current\n\n        p_prev_prev = p_prev\n        p_prev = p_current"}
